  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 1: ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & CORE ANATOMY -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="core-foundations" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-primary">&sect; 1. ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS: CORE ANATOMY &amp; DESIGN DIVERGENCE</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">The Anatomy of a Scalar Core &amp; Why Open-Source Designs Diverge</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        Before comparing specific microcontroller cores or classifying them into archetypes, we must establish two fundamental truths: <strong>what a scalar processor core is physically made of</strong>, and <strong>why different engineering teams chose radically divergent paths</strong> to implement the exact same RISC-V specification.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.1 UNIVERSAL BUILDING BLOCKS -->
    <div class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-ink">1.1 THE SIX UNIVERSAL BUILDING BLOCKS</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Anatomy of an Embedded Scalar RISC-V Core</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        At the architectural level, every 32-bit scalar RISC-V processor (whether consuming 2 kGE or 50 kGE) executes instructions using an interconnected loop of six primary hardware primitives. The fundamental differences between cores lie not in <em>what</em> blocks exist, but in <em>how wide</em> they are, <em>how many clock cycles</em> they take, and <em>how many are executed concurrently</em>:
      </p>

