|  | | Port W Commit | RAW Bypass | |   | | Branch Flags (SLT, BEQ, BNE, BLT) | |   | | PMP Checking &amp; AHB/AXI Master| |  |
|  | +----------------------------+ |   | +-----------------------------------+ |   | +------------------------------+ |  |
|  +--------------------------------+   +---------------------------------------+   +----------------------------------+  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
          </pre>
        </details>

        <p class="schematic-caption">
          <strong>Figure 1.0:</strong> Master architectural datapath and control topology of a baseline scalar RISC-V (RV32I/E) processor core rendered in SCORE Technical CAD specification. The architecture interconnects the six universal primitives across the high-bandwidth central datapath highway: Block 01 Instruction Fetch (Cyan), Block 02 Decode &amp; ImmGen (Yellow), Block 06 Privileged CSR &amp; Traps (Lavender), Block 03 General-Purpose Register File (Sage), Block 04 Arithmetic Logic Unit &amp; Comparator (Cyan), and Block 05 Load/Store Unit &amp; System Bus (Coral).
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Bridge to Primitive Breakdown -->
      <div class="anatomy-breakdown-intro">
        <h4 class="anatomy-subheading">Deconstructing the Six Universal Building Blocks</h4>
        <p class="ladder-narrative" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          Every block in the architectural schematic above plays a vital, non-negotiable role in instruction execution. Below is a detailed deconstruction of their physical silicon implementation, critical timing paths, and how open-source designs diverge across the performance spectrum:
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- The Six Universal Primitives Top-Down Flow -->
      <div class="primitives-flow">
        <!-- Primitive 1 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 01</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Instruction Fetch &amp; Program Counter (IF &amp; PC)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Maintains the 32-bit memory address of the current instruction word. On every instruction cycle, the PC increments by 4 bytes (or 2 bytes when compressed <code>RVC</code> instructions are enabled). On a branch, jump, or exception, the PC multiplexer redirects execution to the target address.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Dedicated 32-bit PC register, incrementer adder (+4 / +2), target branch redirect mux, and fetch buffer FIFO.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Minimal cores (SERV, PicoRV32) multiplex the main ALU to save an adder; pipelined cores (Ibex, Hazard3) use decoupled prefetch buffers.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 2 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 02</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Instruction Decoder &amp; Immediate Generator (ID &amp; ImmGen)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Slices the 32-bit instruction word into source register indices (<code>rs1</code>, <code>rs2</code>), the destination register index (<code>rd</code>), and the opcode. It signs-extends immediate offsets across I, S, B, U, and J instruction formats and generates internal control signals for the ALU, memory bus, and writeback logic.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Combinational steering multiplexers, sign-extension bit fan-outs, and hazard detection logic.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">In 1&ndash;2 stage cores, ID and EX are merged in a single clock cycle; in 3&ndash;5 stage cores, a pipeline register breaks the critical path.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 3 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 03</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">General-Purpose Register File (RF &bull; x0&ndash;x31)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Houses thirty-two 32-bit registers (<code>x0&ndash;x31</code>) under the standard <code>RV32I</code> specification, or sixteen registers (<code>x0&ndash;x15</code>) under <code>RV32E</code>. Register <code>x0</code> is permanently hardwired to constant 0. Crucially, single-cycle execution mandates <strong>two simultaneous read ports</strong> (for <code>rs1</code> and <code>rs2</code>) and <strong>one synchronous write port</strong> (for <code>rd</code>)—consuming up to 40% of the entire silicon area in compact cores.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Dual-read, single-write multi-ported SRAM or flip-flop array; mapped to distributed LUT-RAM or Block RAM on FPGAs.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value"><code>RV32E</code> cuts registers by 50% to save silicon; SERV collapses registers into 32-bit serial shift registers inside a single FPGA LUT!</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 4 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 04</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Arithmetic Logic Unit &amp; Comparator (ALU &amp; CMP)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              The core computational engine. Performs 32-bit arithmetic addition/subtraction, bitwise operations (<code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>XOR</code>), variable bit shifts (<code>SLL</code>, <code>SRL</code>, <code>SRA</code>), and signed/unsigned magnitude comparisons (<code>SLT</code>, <code>SLTU</code>) for data calculations and conditional branch evaluation.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Parallel carry-lookahead adder (CLA), multi-stage barrel shifter (or serial shift register), and condition evaluator.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Full single-cycle 32-bit barrel shifter (~1.5 kGE) vs. iterative 1-bit serial shifter (PicoRV32/SERV) saving massive gate area.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 5 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 05</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Load/Store Unit &amp; Interconnect Bus (LSU)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Interfaces the internal pipeline with physical SRAM macros, caches, and memory-mapped SoC peripherals. Translates word, half-word, and byte loads (<code>lw</code>, <code>lh</code>, <code>lb</code>) and stores (<code>sw</code>, <code>sh</code>, <code>sb</code>) into bus handshakes, managing byte-lane write strobes and sign-extension for sub-word accesses.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Memory alignment rotator, byte-mask strobe generator, and master bus wrapper (AHB-Lite, AXI4-Lite, Wishbone b4, or OBI).</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Unaligned memory access support (e.g. Hazard3 multi-cycle hardware alignment vs. Ibex immediate exception generation).</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 6 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 06</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Privileged Control &amp; CSR Controller</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Coordinates privileged machine-mode status registers (<code>mstatus</code>, <code>mtvec</code>, <code>mepc</code>, <code>mcause</code>, <code>mie</code>, <code>mip</code>), trap handling, interrupt priority decoding, and synchronous exception dispatching, ensuring cycle-accurate deterministic interrupt latency.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Physical Hardware:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Shadow return PC registers, interrupt masking gates, trap cause encoders, and CSR read/write multiplexers.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Barebones machine-mode timer interrupt vs. enterprise Physical Memory Protection (PMP 16-regions) and Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS).</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.2 THE MODULAR RISC-V ISA SPECTRUM & EXTENSION TRADE-OFFS -->
    <div id="isa-spectrum" class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-purple">1.2 THE MODULAR RISC-V ISA SPECTRUM</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Why Different Cores Implement Different ISAs: Architectural Trade-offs</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        A common point of confusion when evaluating open-source processors is why different cores implement different instruction set subsets: why does <strong>SERV</strong> implement only <code>RV32I</code> or <code>RV32E</code>, while <strong>Ibex</strong> uses <code>RV32IMC</code>, and <strong>Hazard3</strong> adds <code>RV32IMCB</code>? Unlike legacy monolithic architectures (such as x86 or ARM Cortex-M where hardware capabilities are fixed in stone by the architecture profile), RISC-V is <strong>modular by specification</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="ladder-narrative" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        An embedded CPU architect never implements "all of RISC-V". Instead, every architectural extension is an explicit engineering trade-off: <strong>spending physical silicon area (kGE or FPGA LUTs) to eliminate software execution cycles or shrink system-level SRAM die cost</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Sub-part A: Base Integer ISA: RV32I vs RV32E -->
      <div class="isa-subtopic-block" style="margin-bottom: 1.75rem;">
        <h4 class="isa-subtopic-title">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-ink">BASE ISA</span>
          The Register File Dilemma: RV32I vs. RV32E
        </h4>
        <p class="isa-subtopic-text">
          Every 32-bit RISC-V core must pick one of two base integer ISAs: standard <strong>RV32I</strong> or embedded <strong>RV32E</strong>. The sole architectural difference is the number of registers:
        </p>

        <!-- Top-down comparison cards -->
        <div class="isa-base-grid">
          <div class="isa-base-card isa-rv32i">
            <div class="isa-base-card-header">
              <span class="isa-code-pill">RV32I</span>
              <span class="isa-card-title-text">Standard Base &bull; 32 General-Purpose Registers (<code>x0 &ndash; x31</code>)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-base-card-body">
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Hardware Footprint:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val"><strong>1,024 flip-flop bits</strong> + dual 32-to-1 read multiplexers. Consumes <strong>30% &ndash; 50% of total core area</strong> in compact scalar microcontrollers.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Software Advantage:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val">Standard <code>ilp32</code> ABI. Rich register file allows compiler to keep function arguments, loop counters, and local variables in fast registers without stack spills.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Target Niche:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val">General-purpose RTOS (FreeRTOS, Zephyr), compute algorithms, and Linux-capable pipelines (Ibex, SCR1, VeeR EL2).</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="isa-base-card isa-rv32e">
            <div class="isa-base-card-header">
              <span class="isa-code-pill">RV32E</span>
              <span class="isa-card-title-text">Embedded Subset &bull; 16 General-Purpose Registers (<code>x0 &ndash; x15</code>)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-base-card-body">
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Hardware Footprint:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val"><strong>512 flip-flop bits</strong> + dual 16-to-1 read multiplexers. <strong>Slashes register file area by 50%</strong> and eliminates routing congestion.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Software Trade-off:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val">Uses <code>ilp32e</code> ABI. High register pressure forces GCC to spill local variables to the stack in memory, resulting in ~5% &ndash; 10% more memory instructions.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-metric-item">
                <span class="isa-metric-label">Target Niche:</span>
                <span class="isa-metric-val">Ultra-compact silicon envelopes, IoT sensor nodes, and tiny FPGA soft-cores (SERV, DarkRISCV, NEORV32-E).</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Architectural Register Mapping: RV32I vs RV32E -->
        <div class="isa-reg-comparison-box" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
          <div class="isa-reg-header">
            <span class="pill-badge badge-purple">REGISTER FILE COMPARISON</span>
            <h5 class="isa-reg-title">RV32I vs. RV32E Architectural Register Mapping &amp; ABI Conventions</h5>
          </div>
          <p class="isa-reg-desc">
            Both RV32I and RV32E execute the <strong>identical 40 base integer instructions</strong>. The only distinction lies in the architectural register file index space: RV32E restricts register specifiers to <code>x0&ndash;x15</code> (requiring the MSB bit 4 of <code>rs1</code>, <code>rs2</code>, and <code>rd</code> to be <code>0</code>). Any attempt to access <code>x16&ndash;x31</code> on an RV32E core triggers a hardware <strong>Illegal Instruction Exception</strong>:
          </p>
          <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            <table class="data-table isa-reg-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Register</th>
                  <th>ABI Name</th>
                  <th>Saver Role</th>
                  <th>RV32I (32 GPRs &bull; ilp32 ABI)</th>
                  <th>RV32E (16 GPRs &bull; ilp32e ABI)</th>
                  <th>Architectural Function</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>x0</code></td><td><code>zero</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Hardwired 0</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Hardwired 0</span></td><td>Always reads 0x00000000; writes discarded (synthesizes <code>nop</code>, <code>mv</code>, <code>j</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x1</code></td><td><code>ra</code></td><td>Caller</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Return address for function calls (<code>jal</code>, <code>jalr</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x2</code></td><td><code>sp</code></td><td>Callee</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Stack pointer (must remain 16-byte aligned by ABI rule)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x3</code></td><td><code>gp</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Global pointer (points into small data section for 1-cycle access)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x4</code></td><td><code>tp</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Thread pointer (used in multi-threaded RTOS / thread-local storage)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x5&ndash;x7</code></td><td><code>t0&ndash;t2</code></td><td>Caller</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Temporary registers for scratch expressions across basic blocks</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x8</code></td><td><code>s0 / fp</code></td><td>Callee</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Saved register 0 or Frame pointer (tracks stack frame base)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x9</code></td><td><code>s1</code></td><td>Callee</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Saved register 1 (preserved across function calls)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x10&ndash;x11</code></td><td><code>a0&ndash;a1</code></td><td>Caller</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Function arguments 0&ndash;1 and function return values 0&ndash;1</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>x12&ndash;x15</code></td><td><code>a2&ndash;a5</code></td><td>Caller</td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td><td>Function arguments 2&ndash;5</td></tr>
                <tr class="rv32e-omitted-row">
                  <td><code>x16&ndash;x17</code></td>
                  <td><code>a6&ndash;a7</code></td>
                  <td>Caller</td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge fail">✗ Triggers Illegal Inst</span></td>
                  <td>Function arguments 6&ndash;7 (in RV32E, spilled to memory stack via <code>sw</code>)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="rv32e-omitted-row">
                  <td><code>x18&ndash;x27</code></td>
                  <td><code>s2&ndash;s11</code></td>
                  <td>Callee</td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge fail">✗ Triggers Illegal Inst</span></td>
                  <td>Saved registers 2&ndash;11 (in RV32E, compiler spills to stack frame)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="rv32e-omitted-row">
                  <td><code>x28&ndash;x31</code></td>
                  <td><code>t3&ndash;t6</code></td>
                  <td>Caller</td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Supported</span></td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge fail">✗ Triggers Illegal Inst</span></td>
                  <td>Temporary registers 3&ndash;6 (in RV32E, compiler manages with <code>t0&ndash;t2</code>)</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

