    <!-- 1.3 WHY ARE THERE DIFFERENT VARIANTS? -->
    <div id="divergence-drivers" class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-teal">1.3 THE DIVERGENCE DRIVERS</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Why Are There So Many Open-Source Cores?</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        If every core executes the exact same unprivileged RISC-V RV32I instruction set, why didn't the open-source hardware community unite around a single "perfect" CPU? Why do SERV, PicoRV32, DarkRISCV, Ibex, NEORV32, Hazard3, SCR1, VexRiscv, and VeeR EL2 all exist simultaneously?
      </p>
      <p class="ladder-narrative" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        The answer lies in the nature of RISC-V: <strong>the ISA is an open specification contract, not an implementation</strong>. The specification dictates <em>what</em> an <code>add</code> or <code>lw</code> instruction does to architectural state, but deliberately leaves every single microarchitectural decision—datapath width, pipeline stages, memory bus topology, and physical silicon gates—completely open. Different engineering teams face radically different physical constraints:
      </p>

      <div class="drivers-grid">
        <!-- Driver 1 -->
        <div class="driver-card driver-substrate">
          <div class="driver-top-badge">DRIVER 1 &bull; SILICON SUBSTRATE</div>
          <h4>FPGA Architecture vs. ASIC Standard Cells</h4>
          <p>
            An <strong>FPGA</strong> is built from pre-fabricated Look-Up Tables (LUTs) and synchronous Block RAMs (BRAMs). On an FPGA, wide parallel adders and dual-ported SRAMs consume precious logic fabric. Cores like <strong>DarkRISCV</strong> were engineered specifically to exploit asynchronous distributed LUT-RAM for single-cycle execution, while <strong>SERV</strong> strips the CPU down to bit-serial shift registers to fit into fewer than 150 LUTs, allowing hundreds of cores on a single board.
          </p>
          <p>
            Conversely, an <strong>ASIC</strong> is synthesized from standard cell logic gates (AND, NAND, flip-flops) with dedicated static timing analysis (STA). An ASIC designer cannot use asynchronous memory tricks; they need clean synchronous clock boundaries and balanced pipeline stages. Cores like <strong>Ibex</strong> and <strong>VeeR EL2</strong> are tailored for ASIC timing closure and high foundry yield.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Driver 2 -->
        <div class="driver-card driver-language">
          <div class="driver-top-badge">DRIVER 2 &bull; HDL &amp; VERIFICATION CULTURE</div>
          <h4>Toolchain Portability vs. Enterprise Verification</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>Verilog-2001 (SERV, PicoRV32, DarkRISCV):</strong> Deliberately written in minimalist, classic Verilog so that any engineer can compile, simulate, and synthesize them using 100% open-source tools (Icarus Verilog, Verilator, Yosys) with zero commercial tool licenses.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>SystemVerilog (Ibex, SCR1, VeeR EL2):</strong> Deployed in high-stakes commercial silicon tapeouts. They mandate enterprise verification infrastructure: Universal Verification Methodology (UVM), SystemVerilog Assertions (SVA), and constrained-random instruction generators (Google RISCV-DV).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>VHDL-2008 (NEORV32):</strong> Strictly typed, portable, and heavily favored in European aerospace, defense, and safety-critical FPGA systems where formal code certification is required.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>SpinalHDL / Scala (VexRiscv):</strong> Uses software metaprogramming so that pipeline stages, branch predictors, caches, and MMUs are hardware plugins generated dynamically at compile time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Driver 3 -->
        <div class="driver-card driver-mission">
          <div class="driver-top-badge">DRIVER 3 &bull; TARGET MISSION PROFILE</div>
          <h4>Housekeeping Glue vs. Silicon Root-of-Trust vs. Storage IOPS</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>Housekeeping &amp; Glue Logic:</strong> An FPGA designer managing board power sequencing or reading a temperature sensor once per millisecond needs an ultra-low gate count. Throughput is completely irrelevant. SERV (~2.1 kGE) is the ideal choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Silicon Root-of-Trust (RoT):</strong> A cryptographic security processor (e.g. Google / lowRISC OpenTitan) must resist physical side-channel attacks and fault injection. It needs Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS) redundancy and constant-time execution paths (Ibex).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>High-Performance Storage:</strong> An enterprise NVMe SSD controller handling gigabytes-per-second of PCIe flash data requires hundreds of thousands of IOPS. A single-issue core would bottleneck the flash arrays; it requires a dual-issue superscalar pipeline (Western Digital VeeR EL2).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.4 THE CLASSIFICATION LOGIC -->
    <div id="classification-logic" class="concept-step-card">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-yellow">1.4 THE CLASSIFICATION LOGIC</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Why Four Archetypes? The Microarchitectural Phase Transitions</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        The four archetypes presented on this page are not arbitrary marketing labels. They represent <strong>four distinct physical phase transitions</strong> governed by the universal CPU performance equation:
      </p>

      <div class="formula-callout-box">
        <div class="formula-line">
          <code>Execution Time = (Instructions / Program) &times; CPI &times; (1 / <em>F</em><sub>max</sub>)</code>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-explanation">
          <p>To reduce execution time, an architect can only pull two hardware levers: <strong>decrease CPI</strong> (execute instructions in fewer clock cycles) or <strong>increase <em>F</em><sub>max</sub></strong> (shorten the critical path so the clock ticks faster). However, pulling either lever requires spending <strong>silicon area (kGE)</strong>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetypes-rationale-table-wrap" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <table class="ds-table" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid var(--ink); border-radius: 8px;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: var(--paper-deep); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);">
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Archetype</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Governing Physical Philosophy</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Typical Area</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">CPI Target</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Key Microarchitectural Technique</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Archetype 1</strong><br /><small class="text-muted">Minimalist State Machines</small></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Area is the hard ceiling.</strong> Trade time for silicon by reusing a tiny datapath over multiple clock cycles.</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><span class="badge badge-coral">1.0 &ndash; 9.5 kGE</span></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><code>1.6 &ndash; 32.0</code></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Bit-serial 1-bit ALU (SERV) or single shared 32-bit adder multiplexed over FSM states (PicoRV32).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Archetype 2</strong><br /><small class="text-muted">Balanced 2-Stage Harvard</small></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>1-cycle ALU execution without forwarding logic.</strong> Overlap instruction fetch with combined decode/execute.</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><span class="badge badge-teal">18 &ndash; 32 kGE</span></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><code>1.18 &ndash; 1.69</code></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Separate instruction and data buses; branches take strictly 1 penalty cycle (Ibex, NEORV32).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Archetype 3</strong><br /><small class="text-muted">Deep Pipelined Harvard</small></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Break the 400 MHz critical-path barrier.</strong> Insert pipeline registers between decode, execute, and memory.</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><span class="badge badge-lime">28 &ndash; 42 kGE</span></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><code>1.15 &ndash; 1.30</code></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">3 to 5 decoupled stages with full operand forwarding networks to prevent data hazard stalls (Hazard3, SCR1 MAX).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Archetype 4</strong><br /><small class="text-muted">Superscalar Dual-Issue</small></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Break the single-issue CPI floor of 1.0.</strong> Fetch and execute multiple instructions simultaneously in parallel pipelines.</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><span class="badge badge-yellow">~48.5 kGE</span></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;"><code>~1.03</code></td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;">Dual instruction dispatch, 4 parallel execution units, 512-entry BTB, and dynamic Gshare branch prediction (VeeR EL2).</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 2: THE FOUR ARCHETYPES & THE PROGRESSIVE LADDER -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="microarchitectural-spectrum" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-primary">&sect; 2. THE FOUR ARCHETYPES &amp; PROGRESSIVE LADDER</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">The Scalar Processor Design Space: A Top-Down Progression</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        In deeply embedded computing, the central engineering challenge is balancing the eternal hardware triad: <strong>Silicon Area</strong> (<em>A</em>, in kGE or LUTs), <strong>Operating Frequency</strong> (<em>F</em><sub>max</sub>), and <strong>Execution Efficiency</strong> (CPI, CoreMark/MHz). The evaluated open-source scalar implementations span four distinct microarchitectural philosophies, organized below as a top-down progression from the smallest possible silicon footprint up to high-throughput dual-issue execution.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.0 TOP-DOWN PROGRESSIVE ARCHITECTURAL LADDER -->
    <div class="arch-ladder">
      <!-- Step 1: Archetype 1 -->
      <div class="ladder-step archetype-coral-border">
        <div class="ladder-header">
          <div class="ladder-num-badge badge-coral">01</div>
          <div class="ladder-title-wrap">
            <div class="ladder-eyebrow">ARCHETYPE 1 &bull; 1.0 TO 9.5 kGE &bull; ULTRA-COMPACT SILICON ENVELOPE</div>
            <h3 class="ladder-title">Ultra-Compact Bit-Serial &amp; Minimal Multi-Cycle Cores</h3>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ladder-body">
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Problem:</strong> When silicon area is the absolute limiting constraint (sub-10 kGE or fewer than 1,000 FPGA LUTs), conventional parallel 32-bit execution units cannot physically fit. A standard 32-bit dual-read register file and wide ALU alone consume tens of thousands of transistors.
          </p>
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Architectural Solution:</strong> These cores sacrifice single-cycle execution to save silicon. <strong>SERV</strong> strips the ALU down to a single 1-bit full adder and shifts 32-bit data through it 1 bit per clock cycle over 32 cycles. <strong>PicoRV32</strong> uses an FSM to time-multiplex a single shared 32-bit adder across instruction fetch, address calculation, and arithmetic over 3 to 4 cycles.
          </p>
          <div class="ladder-summary-bar">
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Representative Cores</span>
              <span class="lm-val">SERV, PicoRV32, DarkRISCV</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CPI Span</span>
              <span class="lm-val">1.64 &ndash; 32.0</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CoreMark/MHz</span>
              <span class="lm-val">0.03 &ndash; 0.90</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Primary Niche</span>
              <span class="lm-val">FPGA Housekeeping, Enclave Glue</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Ladder Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="ladder-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Trade Silicon Area (+15 kGE) to Eliminate Multi-Cycle Stalls via 2-Stage Harvard Pipelining</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2: Archetype 2 -->
      <div class="ladder-step archetype-teal-border">
        <div class="ladder-header">
          <div class="ladder-num-badge badge-teal">02</div>
          <div class="ladder-title-wrap">
            <div class="ladder-eyebrow">ARCHETYPE 2 &bull; 18 TO 32 kGE &bull; HARVARD MICROCONTROLLER SWEET SPOT</div>
            <h3 class="ladder-title">Balanced 2-Stage Pipelined Microcontrollers</h3>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ladder-body">
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Problem:</strong> Multi-cycle state machines spend 3 to 32 clock cycles on every instruction, resulting in low throughput that cannot service fast real-time sensor interrupts or secure boot cryptographic loops.
          </p>
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Architectural Solution:</strong> Introduce a 2-stage Harvard pipeline (Instruction Fetch &rarr; Execute/Writeback) with separate instruction and data memory buses. Because fetch occurs concurrently with execution of the previous instruction, standard arithmetic completes in exactly 1 clock cycle (CPI ~1.18 to 1.45), while branch penalties are strictly 1 cycle.
          </p>
          <div class="ladder-summary-bar">
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Representative Cores</span>
              <span class="lm-val">Ibex, NEORV32, SCR1 (MIN)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CPI Span</span>
              <span class="lm-val">1.18 &ndash; 1.69</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CoreMark/MHz</span>
              <span class="lm-val">0.89 &ndash; 3.23</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Primary Niche</span>
              <span class="lm-val">Silicon Root-of-Trust (OpenTitan), IoT SoCs</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Ladder Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="ladder-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Deepen Pipeline (3&ndash;5 Stages) &amp; Forwarding Networks to Break the 400 MHz Critical-Path Ceiling</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 3: Archetype 3 -->
      <div class="ladder-step archetype-lime-border">
        <div class="ladder-header">
          <div class="ladder-num-badge badge-lime">03</div>
          <div class="ladder-title-wrap">
            <div class="ladder-eyebrow">ARCHETYPE 3 &bull; 28 TO 42 kGE &bull; HIGH-EFFICIENCY DEEP PIPELINES</div>
            <h3 class="ladder-title">High-Efficiency Deep Pipelined Harvard Cores (3 to 5 Stages)</h3>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ladder-body">
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Problem:</strong> In 2-stage cores, instruction decode, register access, ALU execution, and data bus handshaking all occur in a single clock cycle. This long combinational path limits maximum clock frequency (<em>F</em><sub>max</sub>) to ~300 MHz in standard cell libraries.
          </p>
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Architectural Solution:</strong> Decompose execution into 3 to 5 decoupled stages (Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Writeback). Dedicated pipeline registers shorten critical paths, enabling &gt;450 MHz clock speeds. Full operand forwarding networks bypass data directly from Memory/Writeback back into the ALU, preventing data hazard stalls.
          </p>
          <div class="ladder-summary-bar">
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Representative Cores</span>
              <span class="lm-val">Hazard3, SCR1 (MAX), VexRiscv, RV12</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CPI Span</span>
              <span class="lm-val">1.15 &ndash; 1.30</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CoreMark/MHz</span>
              <span class="lm-val">2.01 &ndash; 3.17</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Primary Niche</span>
              <span class="lm-val">Commercial Edge SoCs (Raspberry Pi RP2350)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Ladder Connector 3 -> 4 -->
      <div class="ladder-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Overcome Single-Issue Theoretical Floor (CPI &lt; 1.0) via Dual-Issue Dispatch &amp; Gshare Prediction</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 4: Archetype 4 -->
      <div class="ladder-step archetype-yellow-border">
        <div class="ladder-header">
          <div class="ladder-num-badge badge-yellow">04</div>
          <div class="ladder-title-wrap">
            <div class="ladder-eyebrow">ARCHETYPE 4 &bull; ~48.5 kGE &bull; DUAL-ISSUE SUPERSCALAR INDUSTRIAL CLASS</div>
            <h3 class="ladder-title">Superscalar Dual-Issue Industrial Embedded Engines</h3>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ladder-body">
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Problem:</strong> A single-issue in-order pipeline has a theoretical CPI floor of 1.0. Even with zero cache misses and perfect branch prediction, it can never execute more than one instruction per cycle, capping throughput for data-intensive storage controllers and high-IOPS storage networks.
          </p>
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Architectural Solution:</strong> Implement a dual-issue in-order superscalar datapath (Fetch 2 instructions, Decode 2, Dispatch to 4 execution units in parallel). Incorporate a 512-entry Branch Target Buffer (BTB) with a dynamic Gshare branch predictor and dual 64-bit memory ports, driving sustained CPI down to 1.03 in mass-production silicon.
          </p>
          <div class="ladder-summary-bar">
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Representative Cores</span>
              <span class="lm-val">VeeR EL2 (Western Digital)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CPI Span</span>
              <span class="lm-val">~1.03 (Dual-Issue)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">CoreMark/MHz</span>
              <span class="lm-val">2.56</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ladder-metric">
              <span class="lm-lbl">Primary Niche</span>
              <span class="lm-val">High-Performance NVMe SSD Controllers</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- SUBSECTION 2.1: ARCHETYPE 1 DEEP-DIVE -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-1-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-coral-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-coral">2.1 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 1: Ultra-Compact Minimalist Datapaths (SERV, PicoRV32 &amp; DarkRISCV)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          When an embedded systems architect has fewer than 1,000 FPGA LUTs or a silicon area budget below 10 kGE, a classical 32-bit pipelined CPU is physically impossible to fabricate. A conventional 32-bit dual-read single-write register file alone consumes over 32,000 transistors; a dedicated parallel 32-bit carry-lookahead adder demands hundreds more. Archetype 1 abandons wide parallel execution in favor of two ingenious architectural paradigms: <strong>Bit-Serial Datapaths</strong> and <strong>Single Shared-Adder State Machines</strong>.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.1.1 SERV DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-coral">CORE 1</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">SERV &bull; 1-Stage Bit-Serial</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~2.1 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Developed by Olof Kindgren, <strong>SERV</strong> is officially the world's smallest 32-bit RISC-V processor. Instead of computing all 32 bits of an operation simultaneously in a single clock cycle, SERV shrinks the datapath down to a <strong>single 1-bit ALU</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Bit-by-Bit Shift Execution:</strong> Source operands in registers <code>rs1</code> and <code>rs2</code> are stored in serial shift registers. On each clock cycle, exactly 1 bit is shifted out from <code>rs1</code> and 1 bit from <code>rs2</code> into a 1-bit full adder slice.</li>
            <li><strong>Carry Recirculation Loop:</strong> The carry-out (<em>C</em><sub>out</sub>) from the 1-bit addition is captured into a 1-bit flip-flop and recirculated as the carry-in (<em>C</em><sub>in</sub>) for the subsequent bit on the next clock cycle.</li>
            <li><strong>Instruction Latency:</strong> A standard 32-bit integer arithmetic instruction (<code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>xor</code>) takes exactly <strong>32 clock cycles</strong> to shift all bits through the 1-bit ALU into the destination register <code>rd</code>. Non-aligned memory accesses and branches take up to 64 clock cycles.</li>
            <li><strong>Why It Matters in Silicon:</strong> Eliminating 32-bit parallel interconnect buses and wide multiplexers reduces the entire core to just <strong>~2.1 kGE</strong> (126 to 150 Xilinx LUTs). Over 100 SERV cores can be tiled inside a single entry-level FPGA.</li>
          </ul>

