        <!-- 2.1.3 DARKRISCV DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-yellow">CORE 3</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">DarkRISCV &bull; 1-2 Stage Async LUT-RAM</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~8.2 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Developed by Marcelo Samsoniuk in 2018 during a focused 24-hour sprint, <strong>DarkRISCV</strong> explores a radically different corner of the scalar design space: how fast and small an RV32I/E processor can be when microarchitected specifically to exploit modern FPGA silicon primitives. Rather than mimicking ASIC-style synchronous pipelining, DarkRISCV is built around <strong>asynchronous distributed LUT-RAM</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Asynchronous Combinational LUT-RAM (SelectRAM):</strong> Unlike standard synchronous Block RAMs (BRAM) which impose an unavoidable 1-cycle latency before data is valid on output pins (<em>T</em><sub>read</sub> = 1 cycle), FPGA Look-Up Tables with internal memory write circuitry (Xilinx SLICEM / Lattice Distributed RAM) support <strong>purely combinational, asynchronous readouts</strong> (<em>T</em><sub>read</sub> &approx; 0 cycles). When an address stabilizes, the memory contents appear on the output wires instantaneously within the same clock period.</li>
            <li><strong>1-to-2 Stage Overlapping Datapath:</strong> DarkRISCV collapses instruction fetch, opcode decoding, and register operand read into a single continuous combinational ripple path:
              <br /><code>PC &rarr; IADDR &rarr; LUT-RAM IMEM &rarr; IDATA &rarr; Decoder &rarr; REGS Read &rarr; ALU Inputs</code>.
              The combinational ALU calculates the result during the same clock cycle, and commits the result into the destination register <code>REGS[DPTR]</code> on the subsequent rising clock edge (<code>posedge CLK</code>).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Branch Bubbles &amp; The 3-Cycle DarkBridge Protocol:</strong> Basic register arithmetic instructions execute with an ideal <strong>CPI of 1.0</strong>. Conditional branches and jumps incur a single-cycle pipeline bubble (via a <code>FLUSH</code> pulse). Off-chip memory accesses (data loads and stores) transition through <strong>DarkBridge</strong>, a 3-cycle bus state machine (<code>IDLE &rarr; ADDR &rarr; DATA</code>) with programmable wait-states. In our real benchmark runs (CoreMark), DarkRISCV sustains an empirical <strong>CPI of 1.64</strong>, delivering <strong>0.6435 CoreMark/MHz</strong> in just ~8.2 kGE of logic.</li>
            <li><strong>The FPGA Native Elegance vs. ASIC Latch Hazards:</strong> DarkRISCV is a textbook demonstration of substrate-specific microarchitecture. On FPGAs, distributed LUT-RAM is free silicon. On an ASIC, however, synthesizing asynchronous dual-read memories requires transparent D-latch arrays or unclocked multiplexer trees that are notoriously vulnerable to static timing hazards, clock skew, and high dynamic leakage.</li>
            <li><strong>Configurable ISA Flexibility:</strong> DarkRISCV natively supports both <strong>RV32E</strong> (16 registers) and <strong>RV32I</strong> (32 registers) via compile-time switches (<code>__RV32E__</code>), and includes an optional hardware MAC multiplier block (<code>darkmac.v</code>) and custom coprocessor interface.</li>
          </ul>

